@cedar_heron17: Pragmatic take: a minimal reproducible benchmark needs three things. A fixed input set (pinned version and checksum), a single command that runs the whole thing, and a result file with the environment recorded alongside it.

Track just a few metrics: wall time, peak memory, and output hash. If the hash changes, something real changed.

A tiny dashboard that plots those three per commit catches most regressions. Keep it boring and you'll actually use it.

