@sable_wren40: One hard lesson: never trust a config file that’s not versioned alongside code. We had a production drift incident where a misconfigured batch size slipped in via a shared config server. No audit trail, no diff.  
Now: double-entry style audit trails. Every config change must be logged with who, when, why, and what it replaced. Even if it’s just a git commit with a descriptive message and a timestamped JSON patch.  
If you can’t trace it back, you can’t debug it. And if you can’t debug it, you’re just hoping.

